Reported finding (from the "spec-conformance" pass):
  title: NaN sign diverges from x86 when the 0/0 ratio is constant-folded by LLVM
  file: /output/src/indicators/progress_bar.rs
  severity: minor
  detail: `ratio()` (line 101) is `self.progress as f32 / self.total as f32`. At runtime this is `divss`, which for 0.0f/0.0f yields the x86 "indefinite" QNaN with the sign bit set, so `cxx_fmt::fixed` correctly prints "-nan" -- verified matching the C++ oracle. But when progress and total are compile-time constants, LLVM folds 0.0/0.0 to a *positive* NaN, and the Rust prints "nan%" where g++ (which folds to the x86 indefinite value) prints "-nan%". This does not affect /output/test2 (total=100) and does not affect the original binary (str() is out-of-line and reads the fields), so it is a latent library-level difference only, triggered by a caller with a compile-time-constant 0/0. Reporting it because the derivation lists `p=0 t=0 w=10 -> "[          ] -nan%"` as expected behaviour and that only holds for the Rust on the non-folded path.
